  "question": {
    "text": "Which of the following statements is false?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Kirchhoff's second law represents energy conservation",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Wheatstone bridge is the most sensitive when all the four resistances are of the same order of magnitude",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "In a balanced Wheatstone bridge if the cell and the galvanometer are exchanged, the null point is disturbed",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "A rheostat can be used as a potential divider",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Let us assume&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is connected balanced wheat stone bridge, such that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math> as shown below,</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0297_74844564f4d1.png\" style=\"width: 170px; height: 199px;\" />&nbsp;<br />&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<br />If galvanometer and cell is exchanged, then circuit becomes as shown in below figure,&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0297_869a4d22c62a.png\" style=\"width: 170px; height: 187px;\" /><br />&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<br /><br />The circuit is redrawn and can be visualised as below figure,&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0297_2aedce77b530.png\" style=\"width: 170px; height: 156px;\" /><br /><br />&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<br />In this case, if it is balanced, then there must be,&nbsp;<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math><br /><br />Equation&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;realises that, both equations are same, which means null point is not affected.</p>"
  }
